    "https://www.mtu.edu/career/students/": {
      "title": "Students",
      "description": "Students | Career Services Skip to page content Skip to footer navigation Menu Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Go Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Search Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Connect with Us Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Career Services Career Services Students Also in this section Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Students We're in this Together Career Services supports you in your career journey. Wherever you are in the process—from\n                           exploring careers, to applying for graduate school, to building your network—we are\n                           here for you, from the day you become a Husky to the day that you retire. We offer one-on-one advising, workshops, industry events, career fairs, and job search\n                           resources to help you achieve success. Walk-in hours are currently available Monday-Friday\n                           from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. Or you may schedule an in-person or virtual appointment with\n                           a career advisor in Handshake . Getting Started A well-written resume is an essential part of your job search. This is how you develop a resume . Build your Handshake profile. Employers on Handshake are looking to hire Huskies—that's you. Upload your\n                              resume and watch for job, internship, and co-op opportunities. Explore our workshops and events . Attend Career Fair . Build your network —80 percent of jobs are a result of networking. We can help you meet people. Make an appointment with a career advisor. \"Without your help, my years at Michigan Tech wouldn't have been nearly as rewarding.\n                           Thank you for the fun events, advice whenever I needed it, and bringing awesome opportunities\n                           to students.\" Erin Murdoch '18 Career Services Social Media Request Students may request that Career Services collaborate on promoting their organization,\n                           enterprise, or class event that is related to Career Education on our department's\n                           social media accounts. Some examples are if you are hosting recruiters, having an\n                           employer info session, a plant tour, etc. Once the information has been received,\n                           a social media image will be created. Please consider sending photos to career@mtu.edu after your event too! We would love to share how the event went. Send your requests to career@mtu.edu so we can get them added to our social media calendar. Off-Campus Recruiting Verification Students may request a verification letter confirming their participation if they\n                                    need to miss class to engage in professional development activities. Fill out the Verification of Participation Letter Request form Contact Us Career Services Administration Building 220 1400 Townsend Drive Houghton, Michigan 49931 Ph: 906-487-2313 Fax: 906-487-3317 Email: career@mtu.edu Hours & Appointments Appointment Hours Virtual and in-person appointments are available most days. Schedule an appointment in Handshake . Walk-In Hours Monday–Friday 11 a.m.–2 p.m. Iris Portrait Booth Hours Monday–Friday 10 a.m.–4 p.m. Request Information Schedule a Visit Give Apply Admissions Undergraduate Admissions Graduate Admissions Global Campus Request Information Schedule a Visit Apply Virtual Tours Study and Live Academics Degrees / Majors Library Athletics Campus and Beyond Events Calendar Recreation Campus Info About Michigan Tech Accreditation Campus Map Jobs at Michigan Tech News Summer Youth Programs Webcams Policies and Safety Campus Safety Information Emergency Contact Information Report a Concern Public Safety / Police Services Student Disclosures Title IX University Policies Career Services Administration Building 220 1400 Townsend Drive Houghton, Michigan 49931 906-487-2313 906-487-3317 career@mtu.edu Instagram Linkedin Youtube Accessibility Equal Opportunity Email the Webmaster © 2026 Michigan Technological University ©"
    },
    "https://www.mtu.edu/career/students/resumes-letters/": {
      "title": "Resumes and Cover Letters",
      "description": "Resumes and Cover Letters | Career Services Skip to page content Skip to footer navigation Menu Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Go Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Search Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Connect with Us Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Career Services Career Services Students Also in this section Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Resumes and Cover Letters Your resume and cover letter are often your first opportunity to make an impression\n                              on an employer. When used together, they help communicate your skills, experiences,\n                              interests, and potential in a clear and professional way. A strong resume highlights\n                              what you’ve done, while a tailored cover letter explains why you’re interested in\n                              the opportunity and how your background connects to the role. Whether you’re applying\n                              for internships, jobs, research positions, or graduate school, learning how to present\n                              your experiences effectively can help you stand out and open doors to opportunities. The Art of the Impactful Resume & Cover Letter How to Make Your Resume and Cover Letter Stand Out Clear Employers often review resumes quickly - like 15 seconds- so clarity matters. Strong\n                              resumes and cover letters use clean formatting, concise writing, and organized sections\n                              that make information easy to find. Avoid clutter, long paragraphs, and unnecessary\n                              details. Don’t feel like you have to tell them everything about you or your experiences.\n                              Decide which experiences to include by prioritizing the most relevant ones as they\n                              relate the job you are applying to. A clear resume and cover letter helps employers\n                              easily understand your background, experiences, and qualifications. Compelling Your resume is more than a list of responsibilities. Think of resume bullet points\n                              as accomplishment statements that describe why your work mattered and what it impacted.\n                              A cover letter helps connect the dots by telling the story behind your experiences,\n                              interests, and goals. Together, they help employers understand not just what you’ve\n                              done, but who you are and what you can contribute. Use this formula to write  strong bullet points: Relevant The strongest resumes are tailored to a specific role. Review the job description\n                              and highlight experiences, skills, and coursework that align with what the employer\n                              is seeking. Employers want to see clear evidence that you understand the role and\n                              have the qualifications to succeed. Small adjustments to your resume and cover letter\n                              can make a big difference in helping your application stand out. Don’t worry about\n                              matching every keyword, but be specific when describing your experience. What about the ATS? It is common for companies to use an Applicant Tracking System (ATS) to organize their\n                              applications. Think of it as an electronic filing system that helps the human resources\n                              department do their jobs efficiently. One of the many features of an ATS is the resume\n                              parser, a software that converts text into searchable data. The most important thing\n                              to know about this feature is that each company uses it differently. Large companies\n                              may use it to sort and rank candidates and create a list of candidates they want to\n                              interview. Others only use it to store your information in their work system. No matter\n                              how a company uses them, there are a few general tips to keep in mind when tailoring\n                              your resume with the ATS in mind. Stick to standard fonts and section headers. Avoid tables, graphics, headers, footers, images - these cannot be converted into\n                                 clear data. Use keywords from the job description as long as you have the experience , and as long as the resume still reads well . In other words, don’t stuff your resume with keywords hoping it will get a higher\n                                 ranking while making it indecipherable to the person who will actually read it. For example, if the job description asks for Python programming, don’t just say “ability\n                              to code”. Specifically list Python in your skills section and appropriate bullet points. Remember, its only function is to convert text into searchable data, so it needs to\n                              be easy to read. From our experience, most companies only rely on human judgement\n                              to make hiring decisions, not the ATS. Using AI AI tools can be helpful for brainstorming, restructuring bullet points, or assisting\n                              you in the tailoring process. However, letting artificial intelligence do all of the\n                              work for you does not help you stand out. In fact, on paper, it can make you look\n                              like everyone else who is using AI to create their documents. The overly formal, robotic\n                              phrasing is very obvious to recruiters (and career advisors), but more importantly,\n                              it often strips away your personal brand and authentic voice. To stand out in a competitive\n                              job market, your application must sound like you. Use AI to ideate, clean up formatting,\n                              and locate skills gaps, but always rewrite the final draft. Make sure your voice tells\n                              the story you want it to tell and convince the hiring manager that there is a real,\n                              capable person behind the paperwork. Getting Started Need help? Here are a few options to get you started. Request Quinncia Make an Appointment Templates Standard Resume Template First Year's Resume Template Cover Letter Template Request Information Schedule a Visit Give Apply Admissions Undergraduate Admissions Graduate Admissions Global Campus Request Information Schedule a Visit Apply Virtual Tours Study and Live Academics Degrees / Majors Library Athletics Campus and Beyond Events Calendar Recreation Campus Info About Michigan Tech Accreditation Campus Map Jobs at Michigan Tech News Summer Youth Programs Webcams Policies and Safety Campus Safety Information Emergency Contact Information Report a Concern Public Safety / Police Services Student Disclosures Title IX University Policies Career Services Administration Building 220 1400 Townsend Drive Houghton, Michigan 49931 906-487-2313 906-487-3317 career@mtu.edu Instagram Linkedin Youtube Accessibility Equal Opportunity Email the Webmaster © 2026 Michigan Technological University ©"
    },
    "https://www.mtu.edu/career/students/networking/": {
      "title": "Networking Tips",
      "description": "Networking Tips | Career Services Skip to page content Skip to footer navigation Menu Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Go Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Search Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Connect with Us Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Career Services Career Services Students Also in this section Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Personal Introduction Body Language Interview Skills Behavioral Interview LinkedIn Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Networking Tips Watch Your Personal Introduction video Watch Your Personal Introduction Expand Hear what employers are looking for in a first impression. Networking is a buzzword that’s everywhere these days. You may wonder what it really\n                           means and how do you do it. Networking is as simple as meeting new friends and then meeting their friends. In\n                           the case of professional networking, you want to meet people who have the power to\n                           give you a job.  And they really want to meet you, because they want to hire you.\n                           People hire people they know and trust. Always be ready for that opportunity. Even in this digital world, personal connections are still powerful. That's why thousands\n                           of company representatives come to campus during Career Fest and Career Fair time.\n                           They travel a long way just to meet you! Personal Introduction Guide First: Develop your personal introduction so you're always ready to meet new people—they\n                                       might be someone who has the power to give you a job, or connect you with someone\n                                       else who does. Network On Campus Career Fest Industry Days, Career Fair Cookout, Tailgate Party Professional development events, including the Etiquette Dinner, Evening with Industry,\n                                             Career Services presentations Become a Career Services Career Ambassador Company information sessions and meet-and-greets Professors and campus staff Student org industry panel and presentations Network Away From Campus Tell as many people as you can that you’re looking for a job: your family, extended\n                                       family, friends, neighbors, fraternity or sorority, acquaintances, former teachers,\n                                       coaches, bosses, co-workers—anyone and everyone. It may seem a bit awkward, but why\n                                       wouldn't you be looking for a job—that's why you're going to college, right?! They'll\n                                       be impressed that you're taking action and will share the news with others. That's\n                                       how networking works. Then, intentionally ask others to introduce you to specific people who work at a company\n                                       or in an industry you’re targeting. Be open—meet and speak with lots of different\n                                       people—you never know how a connection might help you land a job. Also Utilize: LinkedIn—and even Facebook and X Informational interviews and job shadowing National and regional professional organizations and conferences Michigan Tech Alumni Relations Your Fraternity/Sorority or Student Organizations alumni Be Ready When It Works! Updated resume Professional interview attire Confident interview skills (detailed stories about your experiences) References Hours & Appointments Appointment Hours Virtual and in-person appointments are available most days. Schedule an appointment in Handshake . Walk-In Hours Monday–Friday 11 a.m.–2 p.m. Iris Portrait Booth Hours Monday–Friday 10 a.m.–4 p.m. Request Information Schedule a Visit Give Apply Admissions Undergraduate Admissions Graduate Admissions Global Campus Request Information Schedule a Visit Apply Virtual Tours Study and Live Academics Degrees / Majors Library Athletics Campus and Beyond Events Calendar Recreation Campus Info About Michigan Tech Accreditation Campus Map Jobs at Michigan Tech News Summer Youth Programs Webcams Policies and Safety Campus Safety Information Emergency Contact Information Report a Concern Public Safety / Police Services Student Disclosures Title IX University Policies Career Services Administration Building 220 1400 Townsend Drive Houghton, Michigan 49931 906-487-2313 906-487-3317 career@mtu.edu Instagram Linkedin Youtube Accessibility Equal Opportunity Email the Webmaster © 2026 Michigan Technological University ©"
    },
    "https://www.mtu.edu/career/students/jobs-intern/": {
      "title": "Co-ops and Internships",
      "description": "Co-ops and Internships | Career Services Skip to page content Skip to footer navigation Menu Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Go Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Search Clear Search Input Search Category Michigan Tech This Site Programs Search Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Employers How to Hire Huskies Partner Program Current Employer Partners Career Fair Faculty/Staff Integrating Career in the Classroom Presentations On-Campus Jobs Career Champions Alumni Career Fair Events Connect Career Services Blog Staff Listing Data Portrait Lounge Connect with Us Request Info Visit Apply Give Resources for You Prospective Students Admitted Students Current Students Faculty and Staff Alumni and Friends Parents and Family Industry and Partners Tech Links A to Z Campus Directory Canvas Concur Email Experience Safety Data Sheets Website Settings Career Services Career Services Students Also in this section Students Resumes and Letters Networking and Interviews Jobs | Co-ops | Internships Co-ops Internships Student Testimonials Evaluating Job Offers Workplace Adjustment Tips Start Your Career Path On-Campus Jobs Career Fair Events Co-ops and Internships Co-ops and internships are an effective way for students to gain off-campus work experience\n                           that complements their academic education. One of the best ways to determine whether a\n                           major is right for you is to work in that field. Experiential learning is valuable\n                           for career exploration. Find out what you like, and just as importantly, find out\n                           what you don’t like. It can be easy to fit a co-op or internship experience into your time at Michigan\n                           Tech, but please speak with your advisor before applying for co-op. Advisors will\n                           help you fit experiential learning into your course/degree schedule. A co-op is a joint venture between the student, the University, and the employer, and lasts\n                           a minimum of one academic semester. Students earn credit in an online course where\n                           they reflect on professional growth and work experiences while interacting with career\n                           services staff and other co-op students. An internship is arranged by the student and the employer. It typically occurs during the summer\n                           and may be eligible for credit. “My co-op helped me explore a whole new area in automotive engineering. It also helped\n                                    me improve my communication and negotiations skills with suppliers.” Sia Prudhvi Nagireddy Mechanical Engineering Co-op at Daimler Trucks North America - Portland, OR Questions? Email co-op@mtu.edu , call us at 906-487-2313, or visit Career Services for help. We are located in Room 220 of the Administration Building . Have an Internship or Co-op? Let us know about your internship or co-op! Your response is vital for helping current and future Huskies. Share your experience by creating an Experience in Handshake and selecting “Internship & Co-op Reporting” as the template. Request Information Schedule a Visit Give Apply Admissions Undergraduate Admissions Graduate Admissions Global Campus Request Information Schedule a Visit Apply Virtual Tours Study and Live Academics Degrees / Majors Library Athletics Campus and Beyond Events Calendar Recreation Campus Info About Michigan Tech Accreditation Campus Map Jobs at Michigan Tech News Summer Youth Programs Webcams Policies and Safety Campus Safety Information Emergency Contact Information Report a Concern Public Safety / Police Services Student Disclosures Title IX University Policies Career Services Administration Building 220 1400 Townsend Drive Houghton, Michigan 49931 906-487-2313 906-487-3317 career@mtu.edu Instagram Linkedin Youtube Accessibility Equal Opportunity Email the Webmaster © 2026 Michigan Technological University ©"
    },
